1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:04,608
Three teams, three AI providers, three sets of keys, and three

2
00:00:04,608 --> 00:00:05,261
bills.

3
00:00:05,321 --> 00:00:09,049
The invoice arrives, and nobody can say who spent what.

4
00:00:09,109 --> 00:00:12,399
And when one provider goes down, so does your app.

5
00:00:12,649 --> 00:00:17,653
Fix found: LiteLLM, one gateway for more than a hundred AI

6
00:00:17,653 --> 00:00:18,783
providers.

7
00:00:18,843 --> 00:00:22,127
Think of it as a switchboard for your AI calls.

8
00:00:22,187 --> 00:00:27,133
The core is MIT licensed, with over sixty thousand GitHub stars.

9
00:00:27,383 --> 00:00:32,118
Your apps call one endpoint in the OpenAI format, or use it as a

10
00:00:32,118 --> 00:00:33,441
Python library.

11
00:00:33,501 --> 00:00:37,133
It checks the key, sends the call to the right provider, and

12
00:00:37,133 --> 00:00:38,382
logs what it cost.

13
00:00:38,632 --> 00:00:43,578
One. Switch models without rewriting code: the same call reaches

14
00:00:43,578 --> 00:00:47,433
OpenAI, Anthropic or Gemini. You change one name.

15
00:00:47,493 --> 00:00:51,839
Two. Give each team its own key, with its own models, budget and

16
00:00:51,839 --> 00:00:55,929
rate limit. Once a key passes its budget, its requests fail.

17
00:00:55,989 --> 00:00:59,996
Three. Stay up when a provider fails. It retries, spreads

18
00:00:59,996 --> 00:01:04,200
traffic across deployments, and falls back to another model.

19
00:01:04,450 --> 00:01:06,038
Now, the catch.

20
00:01:06,098 --> 00:01:10,358
It's open core: single sign-on past five users, audit logs and

21
00:01:10,358 --> 00:01:12,940
secret managers need a paid licence.

22
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:17,235
Keys, budgets and spend tracking need a Postgres database, and

23
00:01:17,235 --> 00:01:19,737
more than one copy needs Redis too.

24
00:01:19,797 --> 00:01:23,724
And it moves fast: a new minor version every week, so pin your

25
00:01:23,724 --> 00:01:24,439
version.

26
00:01:24,689 --> 00:01:28,203
It's for platform teams sharing many models across many apps,

27
00:01:28,203 --> 00:01:31,230
and Python developers who want one way to call them.

28
00:01:31,290 --> 00:01:35,075
With one app and one provider, a gateway is probably more than

29
00:01:35,075 --> 00:01:35,820
you need.

30
00:01:35,880 --> 00:01:39,231
Want an alternative? Take a look at Portkey Gateway.

31
00:01:39,481 --> 00:01:43,463
To try it, follow the Docker quick start in their docs: it

32
00:01:43,463 --> 00:01:47,766
starts the gateway and a Postgres database. Then sign in to the

33
00:01:47,766 --> 00:01:48,729
admin page.

34
00:01:48,789 --> 00:01:52,419
What problem should we fix next? Tell us in the comments.

